   Here is the resolver library and some tools (including nslookup) forOSK. They were compiled under OSK ver. 2.4 using ISP ver. 1.3 with theGNU gcc2 compiler ver. 2.5.8 and using PD dmake (available in the PDksh package on chestnut.cs.wisc.edu).  I tried to mark the changes by "#ifdef OSK" so you can find them bysearching for the string "OSK". If I had to change the entire file, the original is left with the suffix ".org".  An essential part of the resolver library is select() that can alsowork on socket paths. The Microware implementation of sockets is crippled -it does not support the _ss_ssig, so select() was obtained by using theexisting PD implementation by the Munich TOP group and adding a combinationof events and alarms. This select() works for several standard (SCF, PIPE)paths and for one network (SOCKMAN) path. It can be generalized to many networkpaths by creating a separate event for each path. Right now the callingprogram has to create an event. See the source of nsquery.c to find out howthis can be done.  A good reference on the subject is the book by P. Albitz and C. Liu,"DNS and BIND", O'Reilly 1992. Examples from this book are included herein the subdirectory EXAMPLES.   Several compiled programs are included in directory CMDS. They include:nslookup, nsquery (from the original BIND sources) and checksoa (to checkthe source of authority, from the book quoted above). To use them youhave to create first the file /h0/resolv.conf. Here is an example:;;  /etc/resolv.conf ;domain		desy.denameserver	131.169.200.2    Replace here "desy.de" by your own domain and the IP address by the IPaddress of your name server.  Then you can try:   nsquery ftp.funet.fior   checksoa if.uj.edu.pl  The manual pages can be found in subdirectory MAN in nroff format. I haveadded PostScript versions for your convenience.  To save some space I removed from DOC the texts of RFC's 920, 974, 1032,1033, 1034, 1035 and 1101 included in the original. If needed, they can beeasily found elsewhere.BUGS : - the resolver library works only in the TCP protocol (and now defaults   to it). I could not find the reason why it does not work in the UDP   protocol - it seems that the OSK machine generates port number 0 in   the UDP packet. - nslookup when used in the interactive mode wants sometimes two CR at   the end of line. Even to get to this stage I had to replace by hand   read() by readln() in command.c (which is the flex output from command.l).Andrzej Kotanski(kotanski@zeus02.desy.de)                                       Cracow, Poland, September 23, 1994